{"id":"9f1e22be-b1ce-4957-91a9-eea6d851d5bf","object":"chat.completion","created":1793336488,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Need logger.py header (imports/format constants) before editing.\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 1, 32)['content'])\n```","reasoning_content":"Let me now implement. I need to see the top of logger.py (lines 1-33) for imports and _FORMAT. Let me read it. Also check the exact text in _log_worker to edit.\n\nI have the _log_worker content. I need to change `logger.info(m)` to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nFor environ.py, add a new descriptor. I need to add an EnvVar factory or a lambda. Requirements: unset -> None; set -> int parse, must be >=32; empty string, unparseable, or <32 raise ValueError with variable name in the message.\n\nThe descriptor's __get__ wraps the exception with f\"{self.name}: {e}\", so ValueError from init gets the name. But careful: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") from e` inside try/except TypeError — for ValueError, `type(e)(str)` works fine, so ValueError with name prefix. Good.\n\nBut empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError already. Good.\n\nSo: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_log_max_message_chars)` where the function returns None if v is None else int(v) with validation >= 32.\n\nLet me define a helper:\n\n```python\ndef _positive_int_or_none(v: str | None, minimum: int = 32) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{value} must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return value\n```\n\nThen descriptor: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_positive_int_or_none)`.\n\nNote bool: int parse; \"  \" whitespace? int(\" \") raises ValueError. Fine.\n\nFor logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only max_message_chars param.\n\n```python\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nThen truncation in format(): must truncate the message body after `%s` substitution but before prefix insertion. Approach: use record.getMessage() to get message, truncate, then build a copy? But we can't modify shared record's msg/args.\n\nThe existing format calls super().format(record) which sets record.message (via record.message? Actually Formatter.format sets `record.message = record.getMessage()` and uses it for the format string with `%` operator). That sets record.message attribute on the shared record — that's already happening in original code. We should avoid changing msg/args but setting record.message is what the original does. Ideally, avoid side effects because the same record then passed to a full handler — record.message gets set but the full handler calls getMessage() again which uses msg/args, unaffected.\n\nHmm, but truncation: if I truncate record.message and use it in super().format, super().format computes `record.message = record.getMessage()` itself, overwriting. So I need a different approach: create a copy of the record (logging.makeRecord / copy.copy), set its msg to truncated and args=() and message, then call super().format on the copy.\n\nBetter: \n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        return self._format_limited(record)\n    ...existing...\n```\n\nImplementation for limited path: \n```python\nmessage = record.getMessage()\ntruncated = _truncate(message, self.max_message_chars)\nif truncated is message:  # no change\n```\nActually simpler: always compute message, truncate; if truncated != message, do a copy:\n\n```python\n        msg_text = record.getMessage()\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if len(msg_text) > budget:\n            shortened = _shorten_message(msg_text, budget)\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = shortened\n            record.args = ()\n            record.message = shortened\n```\nWait — copy.copy(record) copies the record shallowly; exc_info/stack_info are shared references, so tracebacks still formatted properly by super().format on the copy. Setting msg on the copy doesn't affect the original. Good.\n\nBut then super().format(copy) computes `record.message = record.getMessage()` = shortened. Good. And prefix logic uses record.message.\n\nThen the multi-line handling: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — with the copy, record.message = shortened. If shortened is empty string... prefix find would be msg.find(\"\") = 0 giving prefix \"\". Fine, no change. Actually careful: if record.message is empty (falsy), original skips alignment. Fine.\n\nLet me write a helper for truncation:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _shorten_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    budget = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    if budget <= 0:\n        return _OMITTED_MARKER[:max_chars]  # hmm, max_chars>=32, marker len 15, so budget >=17 always\n    head = (budget + 1) // 2\n    tail = budget - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck budget 17 for max_chars=32: marker len = len(\"...<omitted>...\") = 3+9+3 = 15. 32-15=17. head = 9, tail = 8. Matches spec. \n\nNote spec says budget minus marker length, head ceil half, tail rest.\n\nAlso careful about args: `record.getMessage()` handles args substitution. When copying and setting msg/args=(), getMessage returns msg as-is. But if msg contains '%' formatting characters that were already substituted... setting args=() means getMessage returns the string unchanged. Good.\n\nActually wait: getMessage: `msg = str(self.msg); if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. args=() is falsy so fine.\n\nEdge: record.msg already a str with '%'? fine.\n\nNow default init: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import: logger.py imports? Let me check top of logger.py lines 1-33. Let me read it.\n\nAlso \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — already handled by `if _default_handler is None`.\n\nLet's read logger.py top."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9975,"completion_tokens":1597,"total_tokens":11572,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1558},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5879},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}